    "agent_last_message": "The tool is in `output/main.py`, with 27 unit tests in `output/test_wordcount.py`. It uses only the standard library.\n\nRun it from `output/` as `python main.py --input FILE --output FILE`, or from the workspace root as `python output/main.py ...`. Run the tests with `cd output && python -m unittest`.\n\n**Verification**\n- All 27 tests pass.\n- The spec's edge cases (empty file, with and without a trailing newline, consecutive newlines, case folding, punctuation) produce the specified values through the CLI.\n- Exit codes 1 and 0 were checked through the real CLI for every listed case. Exit code 2 is covered by a unit test that injects a `MemoryError`, not by an end-to-end run.\n- On a 100 MB ASCII corpus, the lines, characters, words, unique words, and top 10 match an independent `tr | sort | uniq | wc` pipeline exactly. It ran in 4.6 s with a 113 MB peak RSS.\n- A 200 MB random binary file is rejected with exit 1 in 0.03 s, before it can consume memory.\n- Only Python 3.12 is installed here, so I haven't run on 3.11. I found no 3.12-only syntax or library calls.\n\n**Decisions you may want to revisit**\n- **\"Malformed\" means not valid UTF-8.** The spec doesn't define it. A Latin-1 or Windows-1252 file gets exit 1 instead of counts. This is the most consequential choice.\n- **Usage errors exit 1, not argparse's default 2.** The spec reserves 2 for internal errors.\n- **An unwritable `--output` path exits 1.** The spec doesn't list this case.\n- **Word rules follow the spec's wording.** Only the six listed bytes separate words, so a non-breaking space doesn't split a word. Lowercasing is full Unicode. Characters are bytes, so `\u00e9` counts as 2.\n- **A UTF-8 byte-order mark (BOM) becomes part of the first word.** A file starting with `hello` after a BOM counts `hello` and the BOM-prefixed form separately. Stripping a leading BOM is a small change if you want it.",
